# {{team}} — lead (thin pointer, with memory)

Team handle: `{{team}}` · shape `{{shape}}`{{recurring_note}}

Same runtime as `/{{team}}-{{entry_suffix}}`, different launch. Load the
**`team-forge:run`** skill and follow its `references/{{loop_ref}}` loop for team
`{{team}}`. Every fact is read LIVE — never from this file.

**Resolve the hub from the main checkout, not your CWD** —
`dirname $(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)`. A linked worktree
receives no gitignored files, so `tracker/status.json` is *absent* there, not stale.

- `.claude/team-forge/{{team}}/tracker/status.json` — the single runtime surface:
  live state + the authoritative `goal_directive`, plus a design-derived `plan`
  block. Read `plan`, never edit it.
- `.claude/team-forge/{{team}}/design.yaml` — project, constraints, shape, profiles
- `docs/team-forge/{{team}}/contract.yaml` — the problem contract this runtime serves

## Your memory (the reason this agent exists)

The ledger is the team's state; your `MEMORY.md` is *yours*, and it is the one thing
`/{{team}}-{{entry_suffix}}` cannot give you — a slash-command session starts cold every
time. Record what the ledger structurally cannot: which gates are flaky and how you
confirmed it, dispatch calls that turned out wrong, approaches this codebase rejected,
where the real bottleneck sat. **Not** task status, progress, or anything derivable from
`status.json` — duplicating live state is how a memory starts lying.

Read it before the first task; update it at the close. On a recurring workflow this is
what stops each cycle re-deriving the last one's lessons.

> **Staleness:** this file is a baked copy. `tools/forge.py --check
> .claude/team-forge/{{team}}/design.yaml` reports drift; `--resync` regenerates it and
> preserves the ledger. The runtime policy lives in `team-forge:run` and updates with
> the plugin.
